  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe need to find a sequence of coefficients that minimizes the AC1 upper bound, defined as 2*n*||f*f||_\u221e / (sum f)^2. Our strategy uses an LP-based iterative refinement that balances between finding structured step functions (favoring AC1 reduction) and exploring diverse sequences. The core insight is that the optimum often lies in piecewise constant distributions, so we combine an iterative approach with dpprediction techniques to selectively test fractional improvements over the current solution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Input: A sequence length n (192 given a parent sequence)\n- Process: \n  For each iteration:\n  1) Compute current objective f(x) = 2n||x*x||_\u221e / (sum x)^2\n  2) Formulate and solve the supporting LP to find the next sequence\n    maximize_g b\u1d40g\n    subject to:\n      (x * g)[k] \u2264 ||x*x||_\u221e for all k\n      g\u22650\n  3) Update x = (1-\u03b1)x + \u03b1g with amortized adaptive \u03b1\n- Output: Sequence of length n with elements in [0,1000]\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm begins by initializing a solution from the parent sequence (clipped to [0,1000]). The core iterative step involves:\n- FFT-based convolution to compute the convolution supremum \n- Formulating the LP problem with dense but moderate-sized matrices (n x (2n-1))\n- Resolving with linprog to find the next direction\n- Line search with exponential decay to find the best blend\n- Early termination if the improvement falls below 0.1%\nKey optimizations include:\n- Using next_fast_len for FFT convolutions\n- Cache reuse of FFT computations\n- Monitoring solver with 'highs' method and presolve\n- Adaptive line search stopping at minimal improvements\n\n4. Search choices\n- Fixed 3000 total iterations with adaptive step sizes\n- Maintained dimensionality n=192 throughout\n- Used '\\OK' requirement stopping criterion\n- Clipped all outputs to [0,1000] and ensured sum>=0.01\n\n5. Compute budget\nn=192 allows FFT-based approaches (O(n log n)) and dense LPs. With ~3000 LPs:\n- Each LP has 383 (2*192-1) constraints and 192 variables\n- FFT costs ~O(n log n) ~ 3000 ops per convolution\n- Total operation ~90 million, within budget on 1 core\n\n6. Validation\nThe final candidate is re-evaluated exactly using the provided `evaluate_sequence` after final convolution checks. The algorithm recursively compares the best found solution at each checkpoint to ensure optimization.",
